{{Navbar-Global Gas Infrastructure Tracker}}
'''Cimarron River Gas Pipeline''' is a cancelled natural gas pipeline in the United States.<ref name="r30800f">{{Cite web|url=https://www.eia.gov/naturalgas/pipelines/EIA-NaturalGasPipelineProjectsAug2026.xlsx|title=Natural Gas Pipeline Projects, August 2026 release, U.S. Energy Information Administration|website=eia.gov|access-date=2026-11-04}}</ref>

==Location==
The pipeline was proposed to run from Beaver Compressor Station area, Beaver County, Oklahoma to National Helium Gas Processing Plant, Seward County, Kansas.<ref name="r22ec46">{{Cite web|url=https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2017/12/14/2017-26929.txt|title=federalregister.gov|website=federalregister.gov|access-date=2026-11-04}}</ref>

==Project Details==
*'''Owner: '''Cimarron River Pipeline LLC<ref name="r22ec46" />
*'''Capacity: '''631 MMcf/d<ref name="r22ec46" /><ref name="r30800f" />
*'''Length: '''49.3 mi<ref name="r22ec46" />
*'''Status: '''Cancelled<ref name="r30800f" />

==Background==
A FERC notice from December 2017 (docket PF17-7-000) said Cimarron River Pipeline LLC planned about 49.3 miles of new pipe in Beaver County, Oklahoma, and Seward County, Kansas. It also planned to lease 19.1 miles of an idle 26-inch pipeline. About 23 miles of the new pipe would be 20-inch and about 24.3 miles would be 30-inch, with short laterals to the National Helium Gas Processing Plant. The project would add up to 631 million standard cubic feet per day of firm capacity.<ref name="r22ec46" />

EIA lists the project as an expansion sponsored by DCP Midstream, cancelled in July 2018 after the anchor shipper withdrew.<ref name="r30800f" />

